window.AMETHERIAN_SEARCH = [{"path":"/de/","title":"Eine neue Art von Bibliotheks-Ökosystem für Node","section":"Start","description":"Ametherian ist ein unabhängiges Bibliotheks-Ökosystem für Node.js: fokussierte JavaScript- und TypeScript-Pakete, gemeinsame Verträge und sicherheitsbewusste Entwicklung.","headings":[],"text":"Independent Node.js Ecosystem A new kind of Node library ecosystem. Ametherian is a family of modular JavaScript and TypeScript libraries under the @foulfoxhacks npm scope. Ten packages exist as tested previews: focused packages, shared contracts, and security considered from the first design decision. Read the vision → Explore the modules Ametherian A new kind of Node library ecosystem Project status Packages 10 in preview npm release Not published Security audit Not performed Node.js-first JavaScript · TypeScript · npm Overview One ecosystem. Independently adoptable tools. Node.js developers assemble networking, validation, caching, concurrency, observability, and application infrastructure from many packages. Ametherian investigates where consistent contracts and well-defined package boundaries could make that work easier to understand and maintain. Each package has a clear purpose, documented behavior, explicit limits, and the freedom to be adopted on its own. The ambition is broader than replacing a single HTTP client. Read the project vision → Package Map Five core areas. Each family now has preview packages with tests and threat models. See the modules . Networking and protocols HTTP clients, transports, connection pools, proxies, streaming, retries, deadlines, and cancellation. ↗ Data and serialization Validation, schemas, parsing, serialization, conversion, structured data, and safe handling of untrusted input. ↗ Application infrastructure Caching, queues, concurrency, rate limiting, configuration, lifecycle management, and resilience. ↗ Security engineering Securi"},{"path":"/de/docs/","title":"Dokumentation","section":"Dokumentation","description":"Lies die Ametherian-Dokumentation: Projektvision, Paketübersicht, Architekturrichtung, Sicherheitsansatz und Roadmap.","headings":[{"id":"start","text":"Start here #","level":2},{"id":"policy","text":"Project policy #","level":2},{"id":"status","text":"Status at a glance #","level":2},{"id":"conventions","text":"How to read the documentation #","level":2},{"id":"records","text":"Project records #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation Documentation Documentation Vision, package map, architecture, security, and roadmap. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Status. Ten Ametherian packages exist as tested previews in the repository. They are not published to npm and have not been audited. Nothing in this documentation is a release announcement. Start here # Read the documentation in order if you are evaluating the direction. Each document states what is planned, what is not yet known, and what evidence would count as progress. Document Project vision Why an ecosystem, what is in scope, and the boundaries the project will respect. Read → Document Package map Five areas and the preview packages in each. Read → Document Architecture direction Modular packages, shared contracts, runtime boundaries, and resource safety. Read → Document Security approach Threat modeling, secure engineering practices, and vulnerability intelligence. Read → Document Roadmap The staged path from research to release readiness. Read → Project policy # Policy documents describe how the project handles security reports and contributions. Policy Security policy Current reporting status, coordinated disclosure, and the intended security process. Read → Policy Contributing How to give feedback, standards, and pull request expectations. Read → Status at a glance # The table below is the project’s own status report. A row moves forward only when there is public evidence. Ametherian project status by area Area Current status What would establish progress Project vision Published Maintainer review and community fee"},{"path":"/de/docs/vision/","title":"Projektvision","section":"Proposal document","description":"Die Ametherian-Projektvision: eine Familie fokussierter JavaScript- und TypeScript-Pakete mit klaren Grenzen.","headings":[{"id":"why","text":"Why an ecosystem #","level":2},{"id":"scope","text":"What is in scope #","level":2},{"id":"boundaries","text":"What the project will not promise #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Project vision Document Project vision A cohesive ecosystem of focused packages — not one oversized dependency. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Why an ecosystem # Node.js has a mature package ecosystem. It also has recurring friction: overlapping packages, inconsistent contracts, undocumented limits, and security posture that varies by maintainer. Ametherian investigates whether a cohesive family of focused packages can reduce that friction without forcing adoption of one oversized dependency. The goal is not a single framework. The goal is a set of modules that share conventions, document their behavior, and can be adopted independently. What is in scope # The package map groups the work into five areas: networking, data and serialization, application infrastructure, security engineering, and observability. Focused responsibilities. A package should do one understandable thing and remain useful without unrelated modules. Explicit limits. Runtime requirements, side effects, failure modes, and resource bounds should be documented rather than hidden. Security by design. Threat models and safe defaults belong in the design, not in a later checklist. What the project will not promise # Complete vulnerability prevention. Universal compatibility with arbitrary applications or runtimes. Automatic safety for applications that misuse or misconfigure a package. Drop-in replacement of Axios, Undici, or any other existing library. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or a"},{"path":"/de/docs/packages/","title":"Paketübersicht","section":"Proposal document","description":"Die Ametherian-Paketübersicht: Netzwerk, Daten und Serialisierung, Anwendungsinfrastruktur, Sicherheitstechnik und Observability.","headings":[{"id":"networking-and-protocols","text":"Networking and protocols #","level":2},{"id":"data-and-serialization","text":"Data and serialization #","level":2},{"id":"application-infrastructure","text":"Application infrastructure #","level":2},{"id":"security-engineering","text":"Security engineering #","level":2},{"id":"observability-and-developer-experience","text":"Observability and developer experience #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Package map Document Package map Five areas, each with preview packages. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ The package map groups Ametherian into five areas. Each area now has preview packages with tests, type declarations and threat models; see Modules for details. Package APIs may still change until the first stable release. Networking and protocols # HTTP clients, transports, connection pools, proxies, streaming, retries, deadlines, and cancellation. Preview packages: ametherian-http , ametherian-guard . Data and serialization # Validation, schemas, parsing, serialization, conversion, structured data, and safe handling of untrusted input. Preview package: ametherian-schema . Application infrastructure # Caching, queues, concurrency, rate limiting, configuration, lifecycle management, and resilience. Preview packages: ametherian-core , ametherian-cache , ametherian-resilience . Security engineering # Security policy helpers, secure configuration, dependency review, test fixtures, and regression tooling. Preview package: ametherian-security . Observability and developer experience # Logging, diagnostics, metrics, tracing, typed errors, test utilities, fuzzing helpers, and documentation tooling. Preview packages: ametherian-observability , ametherian-testing . Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Project vision Next Architecture →"},{"path":"/de/docs/architecture/","title":"Architekturrichtung","section":"Proposal document","description":"Ametherian-Architekturrichtung: modulare Pakete, gemeinsame Verträge, ausdrückliche Grenzen der Node.js-Laufzeit und Ressourcensicherheit.","headings":[{"id":"modularity","text":"Modular packages #","level":2},{"id":"contracts","text":"Shared contracts #","level":2},{"id":"runtime","text":"Runtime boundaries #","level":2},{"id":"safety","text":"Resource safety #","level":2},{"id":"evidence","text":"Evidence-backed compatibility #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Architecture Document Architecture direction Modular packages, explicit runtime boundaries, shared contracts. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Modular packages # Packages should have one understandable responsibility, declare supported runtime capabilities, and remain useful without unrelated modules. Shared contracts # Shared contracts include typed errors, cancellation, deadlines, resource lifecycle, configuration validation, logging hooks, and documentation of side effects. Shared contracts should reduce fragmentation without circular dependencies or forced dependencies. Runtime boundaries # Node.js is the initial target. APIs should make runtime assumptions explicit and isolate environment-specific behavior behind adapters where useful. Unsupported capabilities should fail clearly. Resource safety # Network and stream-oriented packages should define timeouts, maximum sizes, concurrency bounds, backpressure, cancellation, and cleanup. Defaults should be conservative and configurable. Evidence-backed compatibility # Before stable releases, the project should document supported Node.js versions, module formats, TypeScript support, semantic versioning policy, deprecation windows, and interoperability expectations. Compatibility and performance claims need reproducible tests and benchmark methodology. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Package map Next Security approac"},{"path":"/de/docs/security/","title":"Sicherheitsansatz","section":"Proposal document","description":"Der Sicherheitsansatz von Ametherian: Bedrohungsmodellierung, sichere Entwicklungspraktiken, Schwachstellen-Informationen und koordinierte Offenlegung.","headings":[{"id":"design","text":"Security by design #","level":2},{"id":"threat-modeling","text":"Threat modeling #","level":2},{"id":"practices","text":"Secure engineering practices #","level":2},{"id":"intelligence","text":"Vulnerability intelligence #","level":2},{"id":"disclosure","text":"Coordinated disclosure #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Security approach Document Security approach Threat models, bounded resources, and evidence-backed claims. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Security by design # Security is a design and maintenance requirement, not a marketing guarantee. Each package must document trust boundaries, threat model, assumptions, and known limitations. Threat modeling # Threat modeling should consider SSRF, DNS rebinding, redirect policy bypasses, TLS hostname and certificate validation, credential exposure, unsafe parsing, prototype pollution, decompression bombs, unbounded resource use, cache poisoning and isolation, filesystem traversal, concurrency races, and dependency or build-chain compromise. Secure engineering practices # Reusing mature cryptographic, TLS, compression, and parsing primitives. Bounding resource use and avoiding secret logging. Validating redirects and protocol transitions. Testing malformed and adversarial inputs. Tracking dependency provenance and adding regression tests for confirmed vulnerabilities. Requiring human review for security-sensitive fixes. Vulnerability intelligence # Vulnerability intelligence may draw on OSV, GitHub Security Advisories, NVD, CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities, Node.js security advisories, and upstream advisories. Any ingestion must preserve source attribution, timestamps, affected-version ranges, confidence, and freshness. Intelligence informs review and testing; it must not trigger unreviewed production changes. Coordinated disclosure # Security reports must follow the security policy . Do not post e"},{"path":"/de/docs/roadmap/","title":"Roadmap","section":"Proposal document","description":"Die gestufte Roadmap von Ametherian: Forschung, Spezifikationen, Umsetzung und Veröffentlichungsreife.","headings":[{"id":"research","text":"Research and project foundation #","level":2},{"id":"specifications","text":"Specifications and threat models #","level":2},{"id":"implementation","text":"Implementation and verification #","level":2},{"id":"readiness","text":"Release readiness #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Roadmap Document Roadmap Research, specifications, implementation, and release readiness. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ The roadmap is staged and deliberately avoids dates until scope, ownership, and capacity are established. Research and project foundation # Done. Establish the repository, documentation, and initial research areas. Review existing libraries and identify where consistent contracts would help. Specifications and threat models # Done for the preview packages. Write explicit specifications. Define threat models, resource bounds, failure modes, and support matrices. Implementation and verification # In progress. Ten packages are implemented with tests, fuzzing and a vulnerability model. Independent security review is still required before any package can be described as stable. Release readiness # Not started. A package is not stable or production-ready until its API, support matrix, documentation, tests, security review, release process, and maintenance ownership are explicit. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Security approach Next Security policy →"},{"path":"/de/security/","title":"Sicherheitsrichtlinie","section":"Projektrichtlinien","description":"Ametherian-Sicherheitsrichtlinie: Schwachstellen melden und der vorgesehene Sicherheitsprozess für künftige Pakete.","headings":[{"id":"reporting","text":"Reporting a vulnerability #","level":2},{"id":"scope","text":"Scope #","level":2},{"id":"process","text":"Intended process #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Security policy Policy Security policy Reporting status and the intended security process. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ This policy applies to this repository, including its preview packages, and to future release repositories. Reporting a vulnerability # Do not open a public issue for a security vulnerability. Report it privately to the maintainers. Security-sensitive details must not be posted publicly before coordinated disclosure. Scope # There are currently no released packages and no security-audited guarantees. If a vulnerability is found in the site infrastructure or repository configuration, it should be reported following this policy. Intended process # Acknowledge receipt of the report. Investigate and confirm the issue. Develop and test a fix. Coordinate disclosure and credit. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Roadmap Next Contributing →"},{"path":"/de/contributing/","title":"Mitwirken","section":"Projektrichtlinien","description":"Wie du Feedback zu Ametherian gibst: Standards, Erwartungen an Pull Requests und Links zum Verhaltenskodex.","headings":[{"id":"feedback","text":"Giving feedback #","level":2},{"id":"pull-requests","text":"Pull requests #","level":2},{"id":"security","text":"Security-sensitive changes #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Contributing Policy Contributing How to give feedback on the direction and architecture. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Early feedback on scope, package boundaries, security requirements, runtime compatibility, and developer experience is welcome. Giving feedback # Open an issue to discuss proposals, architecture decisions, or documentation gaps. Keep discussions focused on the technical direction and evidence. Pull requests # Pull requests should be small, focused, and accompanied by a clear description of the problem and the proposed solution. Documentation and tests are part of the change. Security-sensitive changes # Do not post exploit details publicly. Follow the security policy for vulnerability reports and security-sensitive fixes. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Security policy Next Modules →"},{"path":"/de/sitemap/","title":"XML-Sitemap","section":"Site index","description":"Durchsuche die öffentlichen Seiten und die Dokumentation des Ametherian-Projekts.","headings":[],"text":"Ametherian · Site index XML Sitemap Explore the public pages, project policies, documentation, and discovery resources that make up Ametherian. Filter sitemap pages View raw XML Loading the sitemap… Enable JavaScript to display the visual sitemap. The XML sitemap remains available . This is a CSS-styled HTML view of the crawler-facing XML sitemap. The XML remains independently readable by search engines and automated tools."},{"path":"/de/modules/","title":"Module","section":"Module","description":"Die Ametherian-Vorschaumodule: was jedes Paket tut, seine Tests, sein Sicherheitsmodell und die unterstützten Node.js-Versionen.","headings":[{"id":"packages","text":"Packages #","level":2},{"id":"ametherian-core","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-core #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-guard","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-guard #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-schema","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-schema #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-cache","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-cache #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-resilience","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-resilience #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-observability","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-observability #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-http","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-http #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-testing","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-testing #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian-security","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian-security #","level":3},{"id":"ametherian","text":"@foulfoxhacks/ametherian #","level":3},{"id":"vulnerability-model","text":"Vulnerability model #","level":2},{"id":"runtime","text":"Supported runtimes #","level":2},{"id":"status","text":"What preview means #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Modules Implementation status Modules Ten preview packages, tested and unpublished. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ 10 packages 234 test definitions 0 third-party runtime dependencies These packages exist in the repository and pass their tests in CI. They are previews : not published to npm, not audited, and their APIs may still change. They are licensed under Apache 2.0. Packages # @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-core # v0.1.0 · preview Shared contracts: typed errors, deadlines and cancellation, resource limits, redaction, config validation, lifecycle, event hooks, runtime and integrity checks. Tests 74 Depends on — Source packages/core ↗ @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-guard # v0.1.0 · preview Outbound destination policy: blocks SSRF to private, loopback and metadata addresses, checked again on every redirect and at DNS time. Tests 25 Depends on core Source packages/guard ↗ @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-schema # v0.1.0 · preview Validation and bounded parsing of untrusted input, safe against prototype pollution. Tests 13 Depends on core Source packages/schema ↗ @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-cache # v0.1.0 · preview Bounded LRU/TTL cache with load de-duplication and collision-free scopes. Tests 8 Depends on core Source packages/cache ↗ @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-resilience # v0.1.0 · preview Retries with backoff, concurrency and rate limits, circuit breaker. Tests 15 Depends on core Source packages/resilience ↗ @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-observability # v0.1.0 · preview Structured logging with redaction by default, bounded metrics, diagnostics_channel bridge. Tests"},{"path":"/de/changelog/","title":"Änderungsprotokoll","section":"Änderungsprotokoll","description":"Versionshinweise und Build-Verlauf von Ametherian: Versionen, Moduländerungen, Sicherheitskorrekturen und Website-Aktualisierungen.","headings":[{"id":"current-versions","text":"Current package versions #","level":2},{"id":"release-notes","text":"Release notes #","level":2},{"id":"unreleased","text":"Unreleased #","level":2},{"id":"added","text":"Added #","level":3},{"id":"changed","text":"Changed #","level":3},{"id":"removed","text":"Removed #","level":3},{"id":"0-1-0-2026-11-24","text":"0.1.0 — 2026-11-24 #","level":2},{"id":"added","text":"Added #","level":3},{"id":"security","text":"Security #","level":3},{"id":"0-0-0-2026-11-23","text":"0.0.0 — 2026-11-23 #","level":2},{"id":"added","text":"Added #","level":3}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Changelog Releases Changelog Versions, builds, and release notes. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Every push to main that passes CI is published as a numbered preview build on GitHub Releases ↗ , with these notes and per-package tarballs. Versions follow Semantic Versioning ↗ . Build numbers are added as -build.N pre-release tags. Current package versions # @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-core 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-guard 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-schema 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-cache 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-resilience 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-observability 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-http 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-testing 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian-security 0.1.0 @foulfoxhacks/ametherian 0.1.0 Release notes # Release notes are kept in English so they match the tagged builds exactly. All notable changes to Ametherian are recorded here. The format follows Keep a Changelog and versions follow Semantic Versioning . Every push to main that passes CI is published as a numbered preview build ( vX.Y.Z-build.N ) on GitHub Releases with these notes. Packages are unpublished previews until a license is chosen. Unreleased # Added # License: the project and every package are licensed under the Apache License 2.0 ( LICENSE , NOTICE , also shipped in each package tarball). Site: light and dark themes with a System / Light / Dark switch, a mobile pass across every page, and full translations into Español, Português (Brasil), Deutsch, Français, Русский, 日本語 and 简体中文 with hreflang alternates. Site: /sitemap.xml is now "}];
